.com/bullpen/Ed_Hug"}]}, "Edward Ambrose Hug made his only major league appearance on July 6, 1903, as a catcher for the Brooklyn Superbas. The game was the second half of a doubleheader in Cincinnati, and he entered as a replacement for a tired starter in the fifth inning. He drew a walk in his one plate appearance, and the game was stopped in the seventh so Brooklyn could make its train. He was a local amateur catcher at the time and was called upon to relieve a fatigued Lew Ritter in the fifth inning. Per Baseball-Reference-derived data, his debut and final game were both July 6, 1903.\n\nHug's brief career is notable. He shares the major league records for the shortest name and the shortest career. He was born July 14, 1880, in Fayetteville, Ohio, and died May 11, 1953, in Cincinnati, where he is buried. One researcher says Hug's family came from Alsace, and the name was previously Hugh."], "durationSeconds": 5.5235150420000005, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_018q3mu2qqba539Zh7AEhEug", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
